{{also|'midst}}
==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|midest|middest|middis|middst|myddest|mydest|myddis|myddst|mydst}} {{q|all obsolete}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|middes}}, {{m|enm|midst}}, {{m|enm|myddest|t=middle}}, from {{der|en|ang|midde}}, reshaped in Middle English phrases like {{m|enm||in middes|t=in the middle}} by analogy with adverbs in {{m|enm|-s|-(e)s}}; also compare Old English {{m|ang|on middan}}, {{m|ang|tōmiddes}}. Forms in ''-(e)st'' are probably due to influence of superlatives.<ref>{{R:MED Online|middes|n|MED27664}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/mɪdst/|[mɪdst]|[mɪtst]}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-midst.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪdst|s=1}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|often|literary}} A place in the [[middle]] of something; ''may be used of a literal or metaphorical location''.
#* {{RQ:Orczy Miss Elliott|chapter=The Affair at the Novelty Theatre|passage=Miss Phyllis Morgan, as the hapless heroine dressed in the shabbiest of clothes, appears in the '''midst''' of a gay and giddy throng; she apostrophises all and sundry there, including the villain, and has a magnificent scene which always brings down the house, and nightly adds to her histrionic laurels.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1995|author=Mary Ellen Pitts|title=Toward a Dialogue of Understandings: Loren Eiseley and the Critique of Science|pageurl=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=00JEA70gL0AC&pg=PA225&dq=%22midst%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=1H9HVK2UHOK7mQWOp4K4Dw&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22midst%22&f=false|page=225|passage=At dawn, in the '''midst''' of a mist that is both literal and the unformed shifting of thought, he encounters a young fox pup playfully shaking a bone.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2002|author=Nathan W. Schlueter|quoted_in=''One Dream Or Two?: Justice in America and in the Thought of Martin Luther King, Jr.''
|pageurl=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=K-jumkcnVfEC&pg=PA8|page=89|others=1963, speech|quotee=w:Martin Luther King, Jr.|title=w:I Have a Dream|passage=As he said in "I Have a Dream," the Negro "lives on a lonely island of poverty in the '''midst''' of a vast ocean of material prosperity."}}

